with wind speeds topping 250 kilometers  per hour  hurricane Ian ripped across Florida's  Gulf Coast Wednesday afternoon  waves turned streets into rivers  Florida's governor warning the worst may  still be to come it is going to have  major major impacts in terms of wind in  terms of rain in terms of flooding so  this is going to be a nasty nasty day  two days tens of thousands of homes are  without power as utility workers from 27  States prepare to descend as soon as the  storm clears about two and a half  million people were ordered to evacuate  but some stayed behind risking  everything it's my home  it's the only thing I have  I mean and my mother you know I mean  if that goes then  what what do you do it's been decades  since a hurricane of this magnitude hit  Florida's western coast curiosity drew  some people out onto an exposed seabed  earlier Wednesday the water temporarily  sucked away by the storm's low pressure  it is so dangerous to be out there so  even if you see the water receding it's  not the time to go out there and and  look at it or collect shells or whatever  it is the National Hurricane Center is  warning of catastrophic storm surge  winds and flooding across Florida as  people sheltered in place President Joe  Biden said the federal government would  help with certain rescue and Recovery he  is also warning the oil and gas industry  not to raise gas prices  do not  let me repeat  do not use this an excuse to raise  gasoline prices or guys the American  people Ian's slow movement across  Florida means the state will likely be  pummeled for days to come  Heidi Joe Castro Al Jazeera
 

